      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-337099",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$x^{2}ydx - (x^{3} + 2y^{4})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y} - \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}) = \\frac{4}{y}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x, y) = x^{2}y, Q(x, y) =-x^{3} - 2y^{4}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(y) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."
